// A-Level Sociology. One starter for every category introduced by
// socMisconceptionTags in data/forge-data.js. soc is in TAG_TAXONOMY_SUBJECTS,
// so the NO STARTER rule applies to it: every aggregatable tag must have one.
var MC_SOC_HAND_AUTHORED = {
  "MC-SOC-CONJUGAL":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is the Symmetrical Family Symmetrical?",instruction:"Young and Willmott claimed the family had become symmetrical. Mark each piece of evidence as SUPPORTING or CHALLENGING that claim, then write a one-sentence judgement.",items:["Men now do more domestic work than their fathers did","Women in paid work still do most of the housework and childcare","Couples increasingly pool their income in a joint account","Duncombe and Marsden found women also perform 'emotion work'","Men's domestic tasks cluster in cooking and childcare, not cleaning or laundry"],answer:"Supporting: men doing more than their fathers, and income pooling. Challenging: the dual burden, emotion work — the TRIPLE SHIFT of paid work, housework and emotional labour — and the fact that men's contribution is concentrated in the more pleasant and visible tasks. Judgement: the direction of travel is towards symmetry, but 'symmetrical' overstates it; roles have become less segregated without becoming equal. The distinction students must hold: 'more equal than before' and 'equal' are different claims, and Young and Willmott's march of progress thesis is criticised precisely for sliding between them. Note also that pooling income does not prove equal CONTROL of it — Pahl's work on money management shows men often retain decision-making power over pooled resources."},
  "MC-SOC-FAMCHANGE":{type:"distinguish",title:"Decline, or a Different Basis?",instruction:"Giddens argues relationships now rest on a different foundation, not a weaker one. For each feature, state whether it shows relationships WEAKENING or CHANGING THEIR BASIS, and justify.",items:["Higher divorce rates than in the 1950s","Partners staying together only while both are satisfied","People having several long-term partners across a lifetime","Marriage no longer required for cohabitation or children"],answer:"Giddens would call all four a CHANGE OF BASIS, not a weakening. The pure relationship is entered for its own sake and continues only while it satisfies both partners — so divorce rises not because commitment has collapsed but because the reason for staying has shifted from duty and economic necessity to mutual satisfaction. Confluent love is contingent and negotiated; serial monogamy is its predictable consequence, not evidence of moral decay. The New Right read the same facts as decline, which is the debate. The examinable point: the same statistics support opposite interpretations depending on the theory applied, so an answer must state which framework it is arguing from rather than treating rising divorce as self-evidently bad."},
  "MC-SOC-FAMDIVERSITY":{type:"match",title:"Name That Family Type",instruction:"Match each description to the correct family type, then answer: which of these are NEW, and which have simply become more visible?",items:["Few children per generation, several generations living","Relatives living apart but in close contact by phone and visits","Parents organising life around children's needs and schedules","A household whose form is chosen rather than inherited"],answer:"Beanpole family: long and thin — few children per generation but several generations alive at once, produced by falling birth rates and rising life expectancy. Modified extended family: Willmott's term for kin who live apart but maintain close contact. Child-centred family: parents organising around children, linked to smaller family size and a longer construction of childhood. Postmodern family: form chosen rather than inherited, associated with Stacey. Which are new: the beanpole is genuinely new, being a demographic product of the last century. The others are substantially a matter of visibility and definition — Rapoport and Rapoport's five types of diversity make the point that Britain was never as uniformly nuclear as the 1950s image suggests. The error to kill: 'family diversity' does not mean 'more single parents'; it means the nuclear family is no longer the single dominant norm."},
  "MC-SOC-DEMOG":{type:"correct",title:"Birth Rate Is Not Family Size",instruction:"A student writes: 'The birth rate has fallen, which means families now have fewer children.' Explain what the birth rate actually measures, and give two reasons the conclusion does not follow.",answer:"The birth rate is the number of live births per 1,000 of the POPULATION per year — a population-level measure, not a per-family one. Two reasons the conclusion does not follow: (1) it is affected by the age structure of the population, so an ageing society records a lower birth rate even if the families that do have children have the same number; and (2) it is affected by how many people have children at all — a rise in childlessness lowers the birth rate without changing the size of families that exist. The per-family measure is the total fertility rate, children per woman. Both have in fact fallen in Britain, so the student's conclusion happens to be true, but the reasoning does not establish it — and that gap is what the question tests. The related error is treating an ageing population purely as a burden, when it also reflects rising life expectancy and reshapes the family into the beanpole form."},
  "MC-SOC-RELORG":{type:"compare",title:"Church, Denomination, Sect, Cult",instruction:"Complete the table for the four organisation types. Then place these: the Church of England, Methodism, the Moonies, and a New Age healing group.",headers:["Type","Size","Relationship to wider society","Membership demands"],rows:[["Church","","",""],["Denomination","","",""],["Sect","","",""],["Cult","","",""]],answer:"Church: large, closely integrated with and often endorsing the state and wider society, undemanding membership into which people are usually born — the Church of England. Denomination: smaller, broadly accepting of society but without the state link, moderate demands — Methodism. Sect: small, in conflict with and rejecting of wider society, highly demanding and often requiring exclusive commitment, typically with a charismatic leader — the Moonies. Cult: small, loosely organised, world-affirming and individualistic, minimal demands with members often 'customers' rather than adherents — the New Age healing group. The two axes doing all the work are SIZE and the RELATIONSHIP TO WIDER SOCIETY, and the sect/cult line is the one students miss: sects reject society and demand a great deal, cults ask very little and sit comfortably alongside ordinary life. Troeltsch supplies the church/sect distinction, Niebuhr the denomination, and Wallis the world-rejecting, world-accommodating and world-affirming refinement."},
  "MC-SOC-RELFUNCTION":{type:"explain",title:"What Is Religion FOR, Functionally?",instruction:"Durkheim argued religion has a function regardless of whether its beliefs are true. Explain the argument using collective conscience and the sacred/profane distinction, then state what anomie has to do with it.",answer:"Durkheim's claim is that in worshipping a god, society worships ITSELF: the sacred is set apart and treated with awe precisely because it symbolises the collective. Shared ritual reaffirms the COLLECTIVE CONSCIENCE — the shared moral beliefs binding members together — and so reinforces social solidarity and the individual's sense of belonging to something larger. The truth of the doctrine is irrelevant to the analysis, which is what makes it functionalist. Anomie is the condition that arises when that regulation weakens: norms become unclear, and individuals lose the shared framework that gives conduct meaning. Religion, on this account, is one of the institutions holding anomie at bay — which is why Bellah extends the argument to CIVIL religion, where the flag, the anthem and national ritual perform the same integrative work in a secular society. The standard criticism to note: this explains cohesion well but handles religious CONFLICT badly, which is a problem in a religiously diverse society."},
  "MC-SOC-RELIDEOLOGY":{type:"correct",title:"'Opium of the People' — Finish the Quotation",instruction:"A student writes: 'Marx said religion is the opium of the people, meaning it is a drug that keeps people stupid.' Correct the reading, then explain what a theodicy has to do with it.",answer:"The fuller quotation calls religion 'the sigh of the oppressed creature, the heart of a heartless world' as well as the opium of the people — so opium here is an ANALGESIC, not an intoxicant. Marx's point is that religion dulls the pain of exploitation, which makes it both a genuine comfort to the suffering and an obstacle to changing what causes the suffering. It is a criticism of the CONDITIONS that make religion necessary at least as much as of religion itself. A theodicy is a religious explanation of why suffering exists — the promise that the meek shall inherit the earth is the standard example — and on the Marxist account it is the specific mechanism by which religion legitimates inequality, by making present suffering meaningful and future reward conditional on present acceptance. Religion thereby functions as IDEOLOGY: a set of ideas serving the interests of the ruling class while appearing to be simply true. The nuance worth adding is Neo-Marxist: Maduro and liberation theology show religion can also be revolutionary, which the base-superstructure model struggles to accommodate."},
  "MC-SOC-SECULAR":{type:"evaluate",title:"Has Britain Actually Secularised?",instruction:"Sort each piece of evidence into 'supports secularisation' and 'challenges it'. Then state why the answer depends on how secularisation is DEFINED.",items:["Church attendance has fallen sharply since 1960","More people report believing in a higher power than attend any church","Religion has less influence on law, education and politics than in 1900","New Age and holistic spirituality have grown","Religious diversity has increased with migration"],answer:"Supports: falling attendance and the loss of institutional influence — what Wilson and Bruce mean by secularisation. Challenges: belief persisting without belonging (Davie), the growth of New Age spirituality, and increased diversity, which religious market theory (Stark and Bainbridge) reads as competition INCREASING participation rather than decline. Why the definition decides it: if secularisation means falling PARTICIPATION, Britain has clearly secularised; if it means the disappearance of BELIEF, it plainly has not; if it means DISENGAGEMENT of religion from other institutions, it largely has. Davie's 'believing without belonging' and Bruce's counter that vague belief without practice is itself evidence of decline are the two positions to weigh. The error to avoid is treating attendance figures as settling the question — they measure one definition, and a good answer says which one it is arguing about before citing them."},
  "MC-SOC-RELGLOBAL":{type:"correct",title:"Is Fundamentalism Traditional?",instruction:"A student writes: 'Fundamentalism is simply people holding on to traditional religion in the old way.' Give two reasons this is wrong, using the idea of a response to globalisation.",answer:"Two reasons. First, fundamentalism is a MODERN phenomenon, not a survival of the past: Giddens argues it arises as a reaction to the uncertainty globalisation creates, offering certainty where cosmopolitan modernity offers choice. Traditional believers in a pre-modern society had no need to assert their beliefs as fundamentals, because those beliefs were not under challenge. Second, fundamentalist movements characteristically USE modern means — broadcast media, digital organisation, modern political structures — and their literalism about scripture is itself a modern response to modern textual criticism. Bauman frames it as one response to postmodern uncertainty and cosmopolitanism as the other. The examinable consequence: fundamentalism is evidence about the effects of globalisation, not evidence that some societies have failed to modernise — and answers that treat it as backwardness cannot explain why it appears most strongly where modernity is most disruptive."},
  "MC-SOC-MEDIAOWN":{type:"evaluate",title:"Does Owning It Mean Controlling It?",instruction:"State the Marxist and pluralist positions on whether media ownership determines content. Then decide which position each piece of evidence supports.",items:["A handful of conglomerates own most national newspapers","Editors report making day-to-day decisions without proprietor contact","Newspapers compete for readers and reflect their audience's views","Owners appoint the editors who make those daily decisions"],answer:"Marxist (instrumentalist, Miliband): concentration of ownership means a narrow class controls what is said, so content serves ruling-class interests. Pluralist: owners are constrained by market competition, audiences choose what to buy, and editorial independence is real. Evidence: concentration and the power of appointment support the Marxist reading; editorial autonomy and competition for readers support the pluralist. The strongest analysis refuses the binary — the HEGEMONIC approach (Curran, the Glasgow University Media Group) argues that control operates not through direct instruction but through recruitment of journalists who already share a worldview, which is why editors can be genuinely autonomous and still produce predictable output. So 'the owner tells them what to print' is a weak version of the Marxist case, and disproving it does not establish pluralism."},
  "MC-SOC-MEDIAGATE":{type:"distinguish",title:"Deciding What, or Deciding Whether?",instruction:"Label each as AGENDA SETTING or GATEKEEPING, and state the one-line rule that separates them.",items:["An editor decides a story about a minor royal will not run at all","Sustained coverage makes immigration the issue voters name as most important","A story is dropped because it would embarrass a major advertiser","Repeated reporting of knife crime makes it seem the most urgent problem"],answer:"Gatekeeping: the editor dropping the royal story, and the story dropped for the advertiser — decisions about WHETHER something passes through the gate at all. Agenda setting: immigration and knife crime becoming the issues people think matter — the effect of sustained coverage on what the public considers IMPORTANT. The rule: gatekeeping is about SELECTION, agenda setting is about SALIENCE. They are related, since what is gatekept out can never be on the agenda, but they answer different questions. The classic formulation is that the media do not tell people what to think, but are strikingly successful at telling them what to think ABOUT — that sentence is agenda setting exactly. News values (Galtung and Ruge) are the criteria by which gatekeeping decisions are actually made, so a strong answer links the three."},
  "MC-SOC-MEDIAREP":{type:"correct",title:"Representation Is Not Accuracy",instruction:"A student writes: 'A media representation is inaccurate if it does not match reality.' Explain why sociologists do not treat representation as an accuracy question, using stereotype to illustrate.",answer:"Sociologists treat a representation as a CONSTRUCTION — a selective account produced through choices about who appears, in what role, described how, and by whom — rather than as a picture to be scored against reality. The point is not that representations are wrong but that they are MADE, and that the making follows patterns which reproduce existing power relations. A stereotype is the sharpest case: it is not simply an inaccurate belief but a simplified, repeated and evaluative representation of a group that comes to stand in for the group itself. Judging it merely as inaccurate misses that it does WORK — it makes some outcomes seem natural, as with representations of disability as either tragic or heroic, which crowd out the ordinary. The related idea is symbolic annihilation (Tuchman): groups can be misrepresented by ABSENCE, trivialisation or condemnation, none of which is captured by asking whether a given portrayal is factually correct."},
  "MC-SOC-MORALPANIC":{type:"chain",title:"Build the Moral Panic",instruction:"Complete the chain using Cohen's model. Each step must name a specific mechanism, not 'the media report it'. Then say what makes a public concern NOT a moral panic.",prompt:"A group is identified as a threat to societal values → [step] → [step] → [step] → the reaction outstrips the original problem.",answer:"A group is identified as a threat → the media present it in a stylised and stereotypical form, creating a FOLK DEVIL whose image is instantly recognisable → moral entrepreneurs, editors and experts man the barricades and pronounce on the threat, amplifying it → the reaction produces the behaviour it condemns through DEVIANCE AMPLIFICATION, as policing intensifies, more deviance is recorded, and the original prediction appears confirmed. Cohen's Mods and Rockers is the study. What stops a concern being a moral panic: the reaction must be DISPROPORTIONATE to the actual threat, and there must be a clearly identified folk devil. Genuine public alarm at a genuinely large problem is not a moral panic, and the term loses its analytic value if applied to any widespread concern. The evaluation point: critics argue that in a fragmented digital media environment the model works less well, because no single narrative reaches everyone."},
  "MC-SOC-AUDIENCE":{type:"sequence",title:"Order the Audience Models",instruction:"Put these four models in the order they were developed, then mark each PASSIVE or ACTIVE audience. Finally, state which is still taken seriously and why.",items:["Uses and gratifications","Hypodermic syringe","Reception analysis","Two-step flow"],answer:"Order: hypodermic syringe (1930s–40s) → two-step flow (Katz and Lazarsfeld, 1950s) → uses and gratifications (Blumler and Katz, 1970s) → reception analysis (Morley, 1980s). Passive: the hypodermic syringe, which treats the message as injected directly into an undifferentiated mass. Active: the other three — two-step flow introduces opinion leaders who mediate the message; uses and gratifications inverts the question to ask what audiences DO with media (diversion, personal identity, surveillance, relationships); reception analysis shows audiences decode texts in dominant, negotiated or oppositional ways depending on their social position. Which survives: not the hypodermic model, which cannot explain why the same content produces different effects in different people, though it persists in public debate about video games and violence. That gap between the sociological consensus and the popular assumption is itself examinable — and 'media effects' questions are usually really asking you to demonstrate it."},
  "MC-SOC-NEWMEDIA":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is the Digital Divide Just About Access?",instruction:"Decide whether each is a matter of ACCESS or of USE, then judge which matters more as connection becomes near-universal.",items:["A rural household with no broadband connection","A pensioner with a smartphone who uses it only for calls","A student without a quiet space or device to work on","Two users online equally, one researching, one only scrolling"],answer:"Access: the rural household and the student without a device. Use: the pensioner and the contrast between the two online users. As connection approaches universality the divide shifts decisively towards USE — sometimes called the second-level digital divide, where the gap is in skills, confidence and the capacity to convert being online into an advantage. That is why simply distributing devices does not close it, and why the divide tracks class, age and education rather than geography alone. Two related ideas belong in the same answer: cultural imperialism (Sklair) argues global media flows carry Western values one way, though the counter-argument from glocalisation is that audiences adapt rather than absorb; and surveillance, where Foucault's panopticon is used to argue that the visibility of digital life produces self-regulation without anyone needing to watch."},
  "MC-SOC-STRATBASIS":{type:"distinguish",title:"Class, Status, Power",instruction:"Weber argued these are three separate dimensions, not one. For each person, state whether their class, status and power are high or low — and identify the two where the three do not align.",items:["A lottery winner with no education and no social connections","A respected retired head teacher living on a modest pension","A senior civil servant on a middling salary","A wealthy hedge fund manager who is also a peer"],answer:"Lottery winner: high class (market position), LOW status, low power — wealth without prestige or influence. Retired head teacher: modest class, HIGH status, low power. Civil servant: middling class, moderate status, HIGH power over decisions. Hedge fund manager and peer: high on all three. The first two and the third are the misalignments, and they are the whole point of Weber's argument against reducing stratification to economic position: class is your position in the MARKET, status is the social HONOUR others accord you, and power is the ability to achieve your will despite resistance — through the party dimension. Marx treats class as fundamental and the others as derivative; Weber's case is that they vary independently, which is why a wealthy person can lack respect and a respected person can lack wealth. Answers that use 'class' to mean all three cannot describe either position accurately."},
  "MC-SOC-LIFECHANCES":{type:"evaluate",title:"Does Mobility Prove Meritocracy?",instruction:"A commentator argues that because some people from poor backgrounds reach the top, Britain is meritocratic. Give three reasons the inference fails.",answer:"Three reasons. First, ABSOLUTE and RELATIVE mobility differ: the twentieth century saw large absolute mobility because the occupational structure changed — more professional jobs existed, so more people occupied them — without the relative chances of a working-class and a middle-class child becoming any more equal. Second, the existence of exceptions says nothing about the RATE; meritocracy is a claim about how systematically position tracks ability, and a handful of visible cases is consistent with very low mobility overall. Third, apparent merit may itself be inherited: Bourdieu's cultural capital means middle-class children arrive already fluent in what schools reward, so the sorting looks meritocratic while reproducing advantage — what Bowles and Gintis call the myth of meritocracy. The concept that ties these together is LIFE CHANCES, Weber's term for the probability of obtaining valued outcomes — health, education, security. It is a probabilistic idea, which is exactly why individual counter-examples cannot refute it."},
  "MC-SOC-POVERTY":{type:"calculate",title:"Absolute, Relative, or Excluded?",instruction:"Median income is £30,000. The 60% threshold is therefore £18,000. Classify each household, then explain how a country can reduce absolute poverty while relative poverty rises.",items:["Income £14,000, cannot afford adequate food or heating","Income £17,500, food and housing secure but no savings or holidays","Income £25,000, secure, but excluded from local social life by cost","Income £45,000"],answer:"First: absolute AND relative poverty — below subsistence and below 60% of median. Second: relative poverty only, at £17,500 — below the threshold but with basic needs met, which is exactly what the relative measure is for. Third: neither by income, but experiencing SOCIAL EXCLUSION, which is about participation rather than income alone. Fourth: neither. How absolute falls while relative rises: if all incomes rise but top incomes rise faster, everyone can clear the subsistence line while the median moves and more households fall below 60% of it. Absolute poverty measures DEPRIVATION against a fixed standard; relative poverty measures INEQUALITY against a moving one. That is why the two can move in opposite directions and why arguments citing one to refute the other talk past each other. Note the 'underclass' term should be handled carefully: Murray's usage attributes poverty to culture and behaviour, and is contested precisely for blaming the poor for their structural position."},
  "MC-SOC-INTERSECT":{type:"correct",title:"Not Just Adding Disadvantages",instruction:"A student writes: 'Intersectionality means a black working-class woman faces three disadvantages added together.' Explain what the additive model misses, and how patriarchy fits in.",answer:"The additive model treats class, race and gender as separate quantities to be summed, which is precisely what Crenshaw's concept rejects. Intersectionality holds that the categories INTERACT to produce experiences that are qualitatively distinct, not merely more numerous: the position of a black working-class woman is not a white working-class woman's experience plus a racial increment, because the racism she encounters is gendered and the sexism is racialised. Crenshaw's original legal example makes it concrete — anti-discrimination law that recognises race claims and sex claims separately can fail a claimant discriminated against as a black woman specifically, because she fits neither single category cleanly. Patriarchy names the systematic structuring of society around men's power, and intersectionality's contribution is to insist that patriarchy is not experienced identically by all women — which is the black feminist critique of a feminism that generalised from white middle-class experience."},
  "MC-SOC-ELITE":{type:"distinguish",title:"An Elite, or THE Power Elite?",instruction:"Distinguish 'elite' from Mills's 'power elite', then explain what social closure adds. Decide which concept each example illustrates.",items:["The most successful surgeons in the country","Senior figures moving between government, the military and big business","A profession requiring qualifications only certain schools prepare you for"],answer:"An ELITE is simply the top group in any given field — the surgeons. Mills's POWER ELITE is a specific claim: that the leaders of the political, military and corporate spheres in mid-century America formed a single interconnected group, moving between the three and sharing background, schooling and outlook, so that decisions of national consequence were taken by a small, unified and unaccountable circle. The second example is that. SOCIAL CLOSURE, Weber's term developed by Parkin, is the mechanism the third example shows: a group restricts access to rewards by controlling entry, through credentials, licensing or informal networks — which converts current advantage into permanent advantage. The three fit together: elites exist in every field, closure explains how they perpetuate themselves, and the power elite thesis claims that in some societies the separate elites fuse into one. Pluralists dispute the last step, arguing power is dispersed among competing groups."},
  "MC-SOC-CRIMEDEV":{type:"sort",title:"Criminal, Deviant, Both, Neither",instruction:"Sort each act into one of the four boxes. Two change category depending on time or place — identify them.",items:["Parking on a double yellow line","Wearing a swimsuit to a job interview","Killing an enemy soldier in wartime","Cannabis possession","A man wearing a skirt in London","Insider trading"],answer:"Criminal but not deviant: parking on a double yellow — illegal, widely done, rarely condemned. Deviant but not criminal: the swimsuit, and a man in a skirt. Criminal AND deviant: insider trading. NEITHER: killing an enemy soldier in wartime, which is lawful and, in that context, honoured. The two that shift: cannabis possession, criminal in some jurisdictions and not others and policed inconsistently within one; and the man in a skirt, deviant in some societies and unremarkable in others or in other periods. That variability IS the social construction of crime: no act is inherently criminal or deviant, since both are defined by the reaction of others rather than by the act itself. The rule to hold: crime is breaking a LAW, deviance is breaking a NORM, and neither category contains the other — which is why 'crime and deviance' is two topics joined by a conjunction, not a synonym pair."},
  "MC-SOC-STRAIN":{type:"match",title:"Merton's Five Adaptations",instruction:"Match each response to Merton's adaptation, then answer the question that catches people out: does strain theory say poverty causes crime?",items:["Working hard within the rules to get rich","Dealing drugs to achieve wealth","Following the rules with no hope of success","Dropping out of mainstream society entirely","Trying to replace the goals themselves"],answer:"Conformity, innovation, ritualism, retreatism, rebellion in that order. Does strain theory say poverty causes crime? NO — and this is the point students miss. Merton's argument is about the gap between culturally prescribed GOALS (the American Dream, material success, held out to everyone) and the legitimate MEANS of achieving them (unequally distributed). Strain arises from that mismatch, so crime is a response to blocked ASPIRATION, not to deprivation as such. This predicts utilitarian crime by those who want what society told them to want and cannot get it legitimately — which is why it handles theft well and non-utilitarian crime such as vandalism badly. Cohen's STATUS FRUSTRATION fills that gap: working-class boys denied status in the mainstream invert its values and gain status within a delinquent subculture, which explains crime that yields no material reward at all."},
  "MC-SOC-LABELLING":{type:"chain",title:"From One Act to a Deviant Career",instruction:"Complete Becker's chain. Each arrow must name a mechanism. Then state what labelling theory claims about the ORIGIN of the first act.",prompt:"A person commits an act → [step: reaction] → [step: what the label does to identity] → [step: the response of others] → a deviant career.",answer:"An act is committed → an audience with the power to make it stick applies a LABEL, and Becker's point is that deviance is not the quality of the act but the consequence of the rule being applied; moral entrepreneurs are the people who create and enforce those rules → the label becomes a MASTER STATUS overriding all other identities, and the person begins to see themselves through it — the self-fulfilling prophecy → legitimate opportunities close, conventional others withdraw, and a deviant subculture offers belonging → a DEVIANT CAREER, with deviance now organised around the identity. What labelling theory says about the ORIGIN of the first act: almost nothing, and that is the standard criticism. It explains SECONDARY deviance — what follows the label — far better than primary deviance, and can appear to deny the offender agency. Note the contrast with Sutherland's DIFFERENTIAL ASSOCIATION, which does address origins: criminal behaviour is learned in intimate groups where definitions favourable to breaking the law outnumber those against."},
  "MC-SOC-CRIMEPOWER":{type:"classify",title:"Whose Crime Gets Counted?",instruction:"Classify each as white-collar, corporate, state or green crime. Then answer: why are these systematically under-represented in crime statistics?",items:["An accountant embezzling from her employer","A company knowingly selling a dangerous product","A government detaining people without trial","A firm illegally dumping toxic waste"],answer:"White-collar: the accountant — an individual using occupational position for personal gain, at the employer's expense. Corporate: the dangerous product, committed BY the organisation for its benefit. State: detention without trial, committed by or with the authority of government. Green: illegal waste dumping, though green criminology also covers lawful environmental harm, which is why some define it by harm rather than by law. Why they are under-counted: they are less visible and often complex and technical; victimisation is diffuse, so victims may not know they were harmed; regulation is frequently civil rather than criminal, so cases never enter crime statistics; and the powerful shape both what is defined as crime and how vigorously it is pursued — Tombs and Whyte's argument. The examinable consequence: statistics showing crime concentrated among the young, poor and male partly measure WHERE ENFORCEMENT LOOKS, so treating them as a straightforward map of offending builds the bias into the analysis."},
  "MC-SOC-CRIMESTATS":{type:"compare",title:"Two Ways to Count Crime",instruction:"Complete the table for police recorded crime and the Crime Survey for England and Wales. Then state which crimes each systematically misses.",headers:["","Police recorded crime","Crime Survey"],rows:[["How the figure is produced","",""],["Depends on reporting?","",""],["Depends on recording?","",""],["Main blind spot","",""]],answer:"Police recorded crime: produced from offences reported to and then recorded by police; depends on BOTH reporting and recording decisions, so changes in either alter the figure without any change in offending. Crime Survey: produced by asking a large sample about their experience of victimisation; depends on reporting to the interviewer but NOT on police recording, which is why it captures the dark figure of unreported crime. Blind spots: police figures miss crimes not reported — much sexual and domestic violence, and most fraud — and are affected by changes in recording practice. The Survey misses victimless crimes, crimes against organisations, murder for obvious reasons, and until recently under-covered under-16s. Both miss corporate and state crime almost entirely. The conclusion examiners want: the two are not rivals, they measure different things, and the gap between them is itself sociological data about reporting and enforcement — which is why SITUATIONAL crime prevention, targeting opportunity rather than causes, can reduce recorded crime through displacement without reducing offending."},
  "MC-SOC-DEVMEASURE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is Development the Same as GDP?",instruction:"Give three things GDP per capita fails to capture, then name the three components of HDI and explain what the development gap describes.",answer:"GDP per capita misses: DISTRIBUTION, so a high average can coexist with mass poverty; anything unpriced — subsistence farming, unpaid domestic and care work, the informal economy; and everything not economic at all — health, education, rights, environmental degradation. It also counts as positive things that reflect harm, such as spending to clean up pollution. HDI combines life expectancy, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and gross national income per capita, precisely to widen the measure beyond output — Sen's capability approach is the underlying argument that development means expanding what people are actually able to do and be. The DEVELOPMENT GAP is the widening distance between the richest and poorest countries, and the point to make is that it is a gap in RELATIVE position: a poor country's absolute living standards can improve substantially while the gap grows, because richer countries grew faster. Answers that equate development with growth cannot explain why countries with similar GDP per capita differ enormously in life expectancy."},
  "MC-SOC-DEVTHEORY":{type:"compare",title:"Modernisation or Dependency?",instruction:"Complete the table. Then answer: according to each theory, is the West's wealth part of the explanation for poverty elsewhere?",headers:["","Modernisation theory","Dependency theory"],rows:[["Cause of underdevelopment","",""],["Role of the West","",""],["Proposed solution","",""],["Key name","",""]],answer:"Modernisation (Rostow): underdevelopment is caused by INTERNAL obstacles — traditional values, low investment, lack of infrastructure; the West is a MODEL and a source of aid and investment; the solution is to follow the stages of growth towards take-off. Dependency (Frank): underdevelopment is caused EXTERNALLY, by a history of colonial extraction and continuing unequal exchange; the West is the CAUSE, actively underdeveloping the periphery through the metropolis-satellite relationship; the solution is to break the link — isolation, socialist development, or at minimum renegotiated terms. So on the final question the two answers are opposite: for modernisation theory Western wealth is irrelevant to others' poverty and is the destination; for dependency theory the two are causally linked, and the West is rich partly BECAUSE the periphery is poor. Neoliberal development adds a third position — that the obstacle is state interference, and the solution is deregulation and free markets, which is the logic of structural adjustment."},
  "MC-SOC-AIDTRADE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is Aid Simply Good?",instruction:"For each, state one argument FOR and one AGAINST as a route to development. Then say which the dependency theorist would reject most strongly.",items:["Bilateral (government-to-government) aid","Debt relief","Fair trade","Foreign direct investment by a TNC"],answer:"Bilateral aid: for — it funds infrastructure and emergency relief a poor state cannot; against — it is frequently TIED to purchasing from the donor, serves donor strategic interests and can prop up unaccountable elites. Debt relief: for — releases revenue from servicing debts often contracted by previous regimes; against — conditionality can impose the same policies that caused the problem, and it may create moral hazard. Fair trade: for — guarantees a floor price and a social premium, addressing the terms of exchange rather than symptoms; against — it reaches a small share of producers and can keep them in primary production rather than moving up the value chain. FDI: for — brings capital, technology and employment; against — profits are repatriated, TNCs can extract tax concessions and hold more bargaining power than the host state. The dependency theorist rejects TIED AID and FDI most strongly, since both are the mechanism of continued extraction in their analysis — and would note that fair trade, while better, still accepts the division of labour that keeps the periphery supplying primary goods."},
  "MC-SOC-DEVSTRATEGY":{type:"match",title:"Match the Strategy to Its Logic",instruction:"Match each development strategy to the problem it is designed to solve. Then state what sustainable development means beyond the environmental reading.",items:["Microfinance","Appropriate technology","Investment in girls' education","Improving governance"],answer:"Microfinance addresses lack of access to CAPITAL for people with no collateral, extending small loans usually to women — Yunus and the Grameen Bank; the critique is high effective interest rates and evidence that it sustains subsistence enterprise rather than generating growth. Appropriate technology addresses the mismatch between imported capital-intensive technology and local conditions, favouring small-scale, repairable, labour-using methods — Schumacher's 'small is beautiful'. Girls' education addresses gender as a development constraint, with well-evidenced effects on child mortality, fertility and household income. Governance addresses corruption and weak institutions, which neoliberal and institutionalist accounts treat as the binding constraint. Sustainable development beyond the environmental reading: the Brundtland definition is meeting present needs without compromising future generations' ability to meet theirs — which has social and economic dimensions too, covering whether growth is distributed and whether institutions endure, not only whether it is green. The INFORMAL ECONOMY is the context for most of these, since in many countries the majority of work sits outside official statistics and regulation entirely."},
  "MC-SOC-POSINTERP":{type:"compare",title:"What Do They Think Society IS?",instruction:"Complete the table. Then answer the question that distinguishes a good answer: is the difference really about numbers versus words?",headers:["","Positivism","Interpretivism"],rows:[["What society is","",""],["Aim of research","",""],["Preferred methods","",""],["Priority"," "," "]],answer:"Positivism: society is an objective reality with observable patterns and external causes, analysable like the natural world; the aim is to discover cause-and-effect laws; methods are questionnaires, structured interviews, official statistics and experiments; the priority is reliability, representativeness and generalisability. Interpretivism: society is CONSTRUCTED through the meanings actors give their actions; the aim is verstehen, understanding from the actor's point of view; methods are participant observation, unstructured interviews and personal documents; the priority is validity. Is it really about numbers versus words? NO — that is the surface. The difference is ONTOLOGICAL: it follows from what each believes society fundamentally is. Positivists use quantitative methods BECAUSE they think there are external patterns to measure; interpretivists use qualitative methods BECAUSE they think meaning cannot be captured by a tick-box. An answer that says positivists 'prefer numbers' has described the symptom; one that explains why the preference follows from the ontology has explained it, and that is the difference between the bands."},
  "MC-SOC-OBJECTIVITY":{type:"distinguish",title:"Objectivity, or Value Freedom?",instruction:"These are related but not identical. Define each, then decide which is at stake in each situation.",items:["A researcher chooses to study homelessness because she cares about it","A researcher ignores data that contradicts her hypothesis","A funder's priorities determine which topics get researched","A researcher's own class background shapes how she reads an interview"],answer:"OBJECTIVITY is the absence of bias in the conduct and interpretation of research — not letting your preferences determine your findings. VALUE FREEDOM is the stronger claim that sociology can and should be free of values altogether. Situation by situation: choosing the topic out of concern is a values question and, on Weber's account, entirely legitimate — values properly guide what we study, while the investigation itself must be objective. Ignoring contradictory data is a failure of OBJECTIVITY. Funder priorities are a values issue at the level of the discipline, not of the individual researcher. Background shaping interpretation is the reflexivity problem: it threatens objectivity and cannot be eliminated, only acknowledged. The positions to know: Weber separates value-relevance in choosing topics from objectivity in conducting research; Gouldner argues value freedom is a myth that conceals whose side the researcher is on; Becker asks the question directly — whose side are we on? Claiming to have no values is usually a claim to have invisible ones."},
  "MC-SOC-VALREL":{type:"scenario",title:"Valid, Reliable, Both, Neither",instruction:"For each study, decide whether it is likely to be valid, reliable, both or neither. Then state the one-line definition of each that keeps them apart.",items:["A structured questionnaire on drug use, repeated with identical results","A researcher lives with a gang for a year and records what she sees","A survey asking teenagers if they have ever committed a crime","A lab experiment on obedience, replicated many times with the same outcome"],answer:"Structured questionnaire on drug use: RELIABLE but questionable validity — consistent results, but people under-report stigmatised behaviour, so it may reliably measure what people are willing to admit rather than what they do. Year with a gang: high VALIDITY, low reliability — rich, true-to-life data that no one else could reproduce. Self-report crime survey: reliability moderate, validity threatened by memory and social desirability. Lab experiment on obedience: RELIABLE, with validity contested because the setting is artificial. Definitions: RELIABILITY is whether repeating the method produces the same result; VALIDITY is whether it actually measures what it claims to measure. They are independent, and the first two examples prove it — you can have either without the other. The classic quantitative trade-off is that standardising a method to make it reliable often strips the context that would have made it valid, which is exactly the positivist–interpretivist disagreement expressed as a methods choice."},
  "MC-SOC-CAUSATION":{type:"correct",title:"Correlation Is Not Cause",instruction:"A study finds that pupils who eat breakfast get better grades, and concludes breakfast causes attainment. Give three alternative explanations, then state what would be needed to establish cause.",answer:"Three alternatives. (1) REVERSE causation is implausible here but must always be checked. (2) A THIRD VARIABLE: household income plausibly causes both regular breakfast and higher attainment, so the association is spurious. (3) SELECTION: families organised enough to provide breakfast may differ systematically in other ways — routine, supervision, sleep — that affect grades. To establish cause you need, at minimum, a demonstrated correlation, correct TIME ORDER with the cause preceding the effect, and the elimination of plausible third variables — through experimental control, a comparison group, or statistical control. The reason this matters beyond the textbook example: much sociology relies on correlational data from surveys and official statistics, so causal language slips in easily. The disciplined move is to state the mechanism you are proposing and then ask what else would produce the same correlation. Note that establishing cause is a positivist ambition; interpretivists would question whether the framework fits social action at all."},
  "MC-SOC-SAMPLING":{type:"match",title:"Which Sampling Technique?",instruction:"Match each description to its technique, then answer: which of these can produce a representative sample, and which cannot even in principle?",items:["Every nth name from the list","Names drawn at random from the whole list","The list divided by year group and gender, then drawn proportionally","Whoever is available and willing","Participants recruiting further participants","Quotas filled by the researcher's choice of who to approach"],answer:"Systematic, simple random, stratified, opportunity, snowball, quota, in that order. Capable of representativeness: simple random, systematic and stratified — all probability methods drawn from a SAMPLING FRAME, the list of everyone in the population. Stratified is the strongest, since it guarantees proportions on the characteristics that matter rather than trusting chance. Cannot be representative even in principle: opportunity, snowball and quota, because selection is non-random and the probability of any individual being chosen is unknown. Snowball earns its place regardless — it is often the ONLY way to reach hidden populations with no sampling frame, such as drug users or undocumented workers, and its unrepresentativeness is an accepted cost. The error to correct: random does not mean haphazard, it means every member has a known and equal chance; and a sample cannot be representative if no adequate sampling frame exists, however large it is."},
  "MC-SOC-RESDESIGN":{type:"sequence",title:"Order the Design Stages",instruction:"Put these stages in order, then explain what operationalisation actually requires by turning 'educational success' into something measurable.",items:["Run a pilot study","Formulate a hypothesis","Operationalise the concepts","Amend the instrument and run the main study"],answer:"Hypothesis → operationalisation → pilot study → amend and run. Operationalising 'educational success' means converting an abstract concept into a specific measurable INDICATOR — number of GCSEs at grade 5 or above, or progression to higher education, or value-added score. Each choice is a decision with consequences: counting GCSEs measures attainment at one moment, value-added measures progress, and the two rank pupils differently, which is precisely why the operationalisation must be stated rather than assumed. That is the interpretivist objection — that operationalising an idea like success imposes the researcher's definition on it. The PILOT study exists to test the instrument before committing: it catches ambiguous wording, questions people refuse to answer, and timing problems, when they can still be fixed cheaply. Its purpose is to improve the method, NOT to produce preliminary findings, which is the most common misunderstanding of it."},
  "MC-SOC-DATATYPE":{type:"classify",title:"Two Axes, Not One",instruction:"Place each on BOTH axes: primary or secondary, and quantitative or qualitative. The point is that the two are independent.",items:["A researcher's own questionnaire producing percentages","A researcher's own unstructured interviews","Official crime statistics","A Victorian diary held in an archive"],answer:"Own questionnaire: PRIMARY and QUANTITATIVE. Own unstructured interviews: PRIMARY and QUALITATIVE. Official crime statistics: SECONDARY and QUANTITATIVE. Victorian diary: SECONDARY and QUALITATIVE. All four combinations exist, which is the whole exercise: primary/secondary is about WHO COLLECTED IT — you, or someone else for their own purposes — while quantitative/qualitative is about the FORM OF THE DATA. Students routinely collapse them and assume secondary means statistics, which makes personal documents, media content and historical records invisible as sources. CONTENT ANALYSIS is the method that crosses the divide most obviously: applied to media it takes qualitative material and produces quantitative counts, and in its thematic form stays qualitative throughout. Naming both axes for any source is a cheap way to show the distinction is understood."},
  "MC-SOC-OFFSTATS":{type:"evaluate",title:"Are Official Statistics Just Facts?",instruction:"Positivists and interpretivists treat official statistics very differently. State each position, then apply both to the claim that police statistics show crime is falling.",answer:"POSITIVISTS treat official statistics as a valuable resource: ready-made, large-scale, collected consistently over time, allowing comparison and the identification of patterns and correlations at low cost — Durkheim's use of suicide statistics is the founding example. INTERPRETIVISTS treat them as a social CONSTRUCT: each statistic is the end point of a chain of human decisions — whether a victim reported, whether an officer recorded, how a category was defined — so the figure measures those decisions as much as the underlying behaviour. Applied to falling police crime figures: a positivist reading takes it as evidence that offending has fallen; an interpretivist asks whether reporting practice, recording rules or enforcement priorities changed, and notes that the same period saw large recorded rises in offences where reporting was actively encouraged. The strongest answer uses both — and this is why the Crime Survey exists as a cross-check. Marxists add a third point: what gets counted reflects whose behaviour is policed, which is why corporate crime is largely absent from the figures."},
  "MC-SOC-OBSERVATION":{type:"compare",title:"Covert or Overt?",instruction:"Complete the table, then answer: is covert observation ever justifiable, and on what basis?",headers:["","Covert","Overt"],rows:[["Hawthorne effect risk","",""],["Validity","",""],["Ethical position","",""],["Practical difficulty","",""]],answer:"Covert: Hawthorne effect largely avoided since the group does not know it is studied; validity correspondingly high; ethically very difficult — no informed consent, no right to withdraw, deception throughout, and possible participation in illegal acts; practically demanding, requiring a maintained cover, no note-taking, and risk to the researcher. Overt: high Hawthorne risk as behaviour changes under observation; validity threatened by that reactivity; ethically far cleaner with consent obtained and withdrawal possible; practically easier, allowing open recording and questions, though gaining and keeping ACCESS is harder because the group may refuse or manage its behaviour. Is covert justifiable? Defensible where the behaviour genuinely cannot be studied otherwise, because participants would alter it if they knew — Humphreys' Tearoom Trade and Patrick's Glasgow gang study are the debated cases. The judgement is a cost–benefit one, made by an ethics committee rather than the researcher alone, and it turns on whether the knowledge gained outweighs the deception involved."},
  "MC-SOC-INTERVIEW":{type:"distinguish",title:"Structured, Unstructured, Questionnaire",instruction:"For each requirement, choose the best instrument and justify. One is a trap — the obvious choice is wrong.",items:["Comparable answers from 2,000 respondents","Understanding how bereaved parents make sense of loss","A large sample at minimal cost with no interviewer present","Exploring a topic where the researcher does not yet know the right questions"],answer:"Comparable answers from 2,000: STRUCTURED interview or questionnaire — identical wording and order make responses comparable and the study replicable. Bereaved parents: UNSTRUCTURED interview, where rapport and flexibility allow meaning to emerge and sensitive material to be handled. Large sample, no interviewer: QUESTIONNAIRE, cheap, wide-reaching and free of interviewer effect, at the cost of low response rates and no ability to clarify. Exploring an unknown topic: UNSTRUCTURED — and this is the trap, because the scale of the study tempts people towards a survey when you cannot write good closed questions about something you do not yet understand, which is precisely what a pilot or exploratory interview stage is for. The distinction to hold: a questionnaire is a self-completed instrument, a structured interview is the same schedule administered BY a researcher — so they differ in interviewer effect and response rate, not in the form of the questions."},
  "MC-SOC-TRIANGULATION":{type:"correct",title:"Two Methods Is Not Triangulation",instruction:"A student writes: 'The researcher used interviews and a questionnaire, so this is triangulation.' Explain what is missing, and name the types of triangulation.",answer:"What is missing is the PURPOSE. Triangulation is not the use of more than one method but the use of multiple sources to CROSS-CHECK findings — approaching the same question from different angles so that the weaknesses of one are offset by the strengths of another, and convergence increases confidence while divergence reveals something. Using two methods to gather different information about different things is mixed methods, not triangulation. The types worth naming: methodological (different methods), data (different times, places or people), investigator (different researchers) and theoretical (different frameworks applied to the same data). The practical value is exactly the positivist–interpretivist trade-off: combining a survey for representativeness with interviews for validity addresses what neither can do alone. The caution to state: where methods disagree, triangulation does not tell you which is right — it tells you the question is harder than it looked, which is a finding rather than a failure."},
  "MC-SOC-RESEFFECT":{type:"distinguish",title:"Three Different Effects",instruction:"Name which effect is operating in each case. All three are about the researcher's presence, but they are not the same thing.",items:["Workers become more productive simply because they are being studied","Pupils give a middle-class interviewer the answers they think she wants","A researcher's own beliefs shape which observations she records as significant"],answer:"HAWTHORNE effect: the workers — behaviour changes because the participants know they are being observed, named after the Western Electric studies where output rose whatever was altered. INTERVIEWER effect: the pupils — the interviewer's characteristics, class, ethnicity, age, gender, accent, shape the answers given, with social desirability bias the usual mechanism. RESEARCHER effect (or researcher bias): the third — the investigator's own values shaping what is noticed, recorded and treated as important, which is a threat at the analysis stage rather than the data-gathering one. The distinction that keeps them apart: the Hawthorne effect changes the participants' BEHAVIOUR, the interviewer effect changes what they SAY, and the researcher effect changes what the investigator SEES. Responses differ accordingly: covert methods reduce the first, matching interviewer characteristics reduces the second, and only reflexivity and transparency address the third."},
  "MC-SOC-ETHICS":{type:"evaluate",title:"Four Principles, One Hard Case",instruction:"State what each principle requires. Then apply all four to a covert study of a far-right group and say which cannot be satisfied.",items:["Informed consent","Confidentiality and anonymity","Right to withdraw","Protection from harm"],answer:"Informed consent: participants must understand the study's nature and purpose and agree freely. Confidentiality and anonymity: identities and identifying details must be protected in storage and publication. Right to withdraw: participants may leave, and remove their data, at any point without penalty. Protection from harm: participants must not be exposed to risks greater than those of ordinary life, physical, psychological, legal or reputational. Applied to a covert study of a far-right group: informed consent and the right to withdraw CANNOT be satisfied — the method depends on participants not knowing, so neither can be offered in advance, though retrospective consent and the right to withdraw data at debrief are partial substitutes. Confidentiality can be honoured and matters more than usual. Protection from harm cuts both ways here, since the RESEARCHER faces real risk, which ethics committees weigh alongside participant welfare. The judgement is whether the knowledge could be obtained otherwise, and it belongs to a committee rather than to the researcher alone."},
  "MC-SOC-METCONTEXT":{type:"apply",title:"Why Schools Are Not Neutral Ground",instruction:"A Methods in Context question asks you to apply a method to a school setting. Using the PET framework, give one problem SPECIFIC to schools under each heading — not a generic weakness of the method.",prompt:"Method: unstructured interviews with pupils about bullying.",answer:"PRACTICAL: schools run on timetables, so interviews must fit lesson slots or break times, and access depends on GATEKEEPERS — head teachers who control entry and may refuse, restrict or steer the researcher towards cooperative pupils, which biases the sample before it is drawn. ETHICAL: pupils are children, so parental consent is required as well as the pupil's; the power imbalance between adult researcher and child makes genuine consent and genuine withdrawal harder; and a study of bullying may uncover safeguarding disclosures the researcher is obliged to pass on, which limits the confidentiality that can honestly be promised. THEORETICAL: pupils may give socially desirable answers to an adult who looks like a teacher, threatening validity; the setting itself is one where children are habituated to giving expected answers; and a sample obtained through the head teacher will not represent the pupils most likely to be excluded or bullied. The mark scheme rewards specificity: a problem that would apply equally to any interview anywhere earns little, because the question is about the CONTEXT."},
  "MC-SOC-STUDYTYPE":{type:"match",title:"Which Study Design?",instruction:"Match each aim to the design that suits it, then state the main weakness of each.",items:["Tracking the same cohort's attainment from 11 to 18","Studying one school in depth to understand its culture","Testing whether teacher expectations change pupil performance","Comparing attitudes across several schools at one moment"],answer:"LONGITUDINAL study for the cohort — its strength is showing change over time in the same people, and its weakness is SAMPLE ATTRITION as participants drop out over years, along with cost and the risk that early questions become dated. CASE STUDY for the single school — rich, valid, holistic detail, but it cannot support generalisation to other schools. FIELD EXPERIMENT for teacher expectations — Rosenthal and Jacobson's Pygmalion in the Classroom is exactly this, and it buys causal inference in a real setting at the cost of weaker control and serious ethical problems, since some pupils are deliberately disadvantaged. CROSS-SECTIONAL survey for comparing schools at one moment — quick and comparable, but it is a snapshot that cannot establish time order and so cannot support causal claims. The pairing rule: match the design to whether the question is about CHANGE, DEPTH, CAUSE or COMPARISON, and the answer follows."},
};
Object.keys(MC_SOC_HAND_AUTHORED).forEach(function (tag) {
  if (!MC_STARTERS[tag]) MC_STARTERS[tag] = MC_SOC_HAND_AUTHORED[tag];
});

